.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 23, 2026
.Dt DTFL_EMBEDDING 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_embedding
.Nd embedding libdtfl in a host program
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/base.h
.Ft int
.Fn dtfl_set_alloc_hooks "const dtfl_alloc_hooks *hooks"
.Ft void
.Fn dtfl_set_panic_hook "dtfl_panic_fn fn" "void *ctx"
.Ft void
.Fn dtfl_set_log_hook "dtfl_log_fn fn" "void *ctx"
.Ft int
.Fn dtfl_set_thread_hooks "const dtfl_thread_hooks *hooks"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
libdtfl is built to run inside a host program, such as a database
server, that supplies its own memory, error reporting, logging and
threads.
Four process-wide hooks route each of those boundaries through the
host.
Each has a default, so a standalone program needs none of them.
.Ss The four host hooks
.Bl -tag -width "dtfl_set_thread_hooks()"
.It Fn dtfl_set_alloc_hooks
All memory libdtfl allocates comes from these.
The default is the libc allocator.
See
.Xr dtfl_set_alloc_hooks 3 .
.It Fn dtfl_set_panic_hook
Called on a contract violation or an allocation failure.
It must not return.
The default prints to standard error and calls
.Xr abort 3 .
A hook that
.Xr longjmp 3 Ns s
out leaves the worker unusable; see
.Sx After a panic
below and
.Xr dtfl_set_panic_hook 3 .
.It Fn dtfl_set_log_hook
Receives human-readable diagnostics at
.Dv DTFL_LOG_DEBUG ,
.Dv DTFL_LOG_INFO ,
.Dv DTFL_LOG_WARN
and
.Dv DTFL_LOG_ERROR .
The default writes WARN and ERROR to standard error and drops the rest.
This is separate from the structured event logging in
.In dtfl/log.h .
See
.Xr dtfl_set_log_hook 3 .
.It Fn dtfl_set_thread_hooks
Spawn and join callbacks for worker, network and connection threads.
The default is
.Xr pthread_create 3 .
See
.Xr dtfl_set_thread_hooks 3 .
.El
.Pp
All four hooks are process-wide.
They must be installed before the first
.Fn dtfl_execute ,
and the allocation hook before any libdtfl call allocates.
Because the hooks are the whole of libdtfl's writable global state, two
independent
.Fn dtfl_execute
calls can run at the same time in one process once the hooks are set.
.Ss Per-thread state
Almost all library state lives in a worker and belongs to the thread
that runs it: scopes, streams, collections, operators, capabilities,
input handles, probes and traces are worker-owned and never leave their
worker thread.
The one piece of per-thread library state is the buffer behind
.Fn dtfl_last_error_detail ,
which holds the detail of the most recent error reported on the calling
thread.
Worker-owned handles and that buffer are why a host must not move a
worker or its handles between threads; the full contract is in
.Xr dtfl 7 .
.Ss Hosts that control their own threads
A host that cannot hand libdtfl free-running threads has two ways to
embed it.
.Pp
First, it can supply thread hooks whose
.Fa spawn
starts a thread the host manages.
.Fn dtfl_execute
then starts and joins each worker through those hooks.
The hook must start a thread in the same process: the workers of one
.Fn dtfl_execute
share memory.
A PostgreSQL background worker is a separate process and cannot back
these hooks, and a PostgreSQL extension must not let libdtfl start
threads at all, since
.Fn palloc
and
.Fn ereport
are not thread-safe and every libdtfl thread allocates through the
hooks.
So an extension calls neither
.Fn dtfl_execute
nor
.Fn dtfl_diag_server_start ,
and uses the second way.
.Pp
Second, and simplest when the host already has an event loop, it can
avoid
.Fn dtfl_execute
entirely and drive a single worker cooperatively:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtfl_worker *w = dtfl_worker_new_standalone();
dtfl_worker_dataflow(w, "my", tc, build, ctx);
while (dtfl_worker_has_dataflows(w))
	dtfl_worker_step(w);
dtfl_worker_free(w);
.Ed
.Pp
In the second mode no thread is created, so no thread hook is needed;
the host calls
.Fn dtfl_worker_step
from its own thread whenever it chooses, interleaving dataflow progress
with its other work.
.Ss After a panic
A panic hook that
.Xr longjmp 3 Ns s
out, as
.Fn ereport ERROR
does, leaves the worker in an unknown state: the panic can come from
deep inside an operator, with a structure half updated or a mutex held.
After the jump nothing reached from that worker may be used again, not
even
.Fn dtfl_worker_free .
The host discards the worker by releasing its memory wholesale, which
is why the example below allocates in a dedicated memory context: it
resets that context with
.Fn MemoryContextReset ,
which keeps the context valid for the rebuild.
Deleting it instead would leave the process-wide hooks pointing at a
dead context.
On Linux a standalone worker holds nothing but memory; elsewhere its
mutexes may own small libc allocations that leak.
File descriptors the host passed in stay the host's to close.
.Pp
An
.Fn ereport ERROR
raised inside a callback libdtfl calls (an operator function, a sink, the
log hook) unwinds through libdtfl the same way, so the log hook maps
.Dv DTFL_LOG_ERROR
to
.Dv LOG ,
and a callback that can fail catches its own errors and returns
normally.
A long-lived worker that holds many views loses them all to one error,
so keep independent dataflows in separate workers, each with its own
memory context, and make the rebuild path the one used after a restart.
The hooks hold the address of a context variable rather than a context,
so the host selects a worker's context by setting the variable before
each call into that worker; create every object a worker uses while its
context is selected, and share none across contexts.
.Ss What libdtfl never does
libdtfl installs no signal handlers and calls no
.Xr getenv 3 ,
.Xr signal 3
or
.Xr getopt 3 .
Environment lookup, when a host wants it, happens only through a
callback the host supplies to
.Fn dtfl_config_from_env .
This keeps the library safe to load into a host that owns those
facilities itself.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A PostgreSQL extension wires every boundary to the server before
starting a computation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* The context libdtfl allocates in; set before each call into a worker. */
static MemoryContext dtfl_cxt;

static void *pg_malloc(size_t n, void *c)
{ return MemoryContextAllocHuge(*(MemoryContext *)c, n); }
static void *pg_realloc(void *p, size_t n, void *c)
{ return p ? repalloc_huge(p, n)
           : MemoryContextAllocHuge(*(MemoryContext *)c, n); }
static void pg_free(void *p, void *c) { pfree(p); }

static void pg_panic(const char *msg, void *c)
{ ereport(ERROR, errmsg("dtfl: %s", msg)); }   /* longjmp, no return */

/* never elog(ERROR) from here: it would unwind through libdtfl */
static void pg_log(dtfl_log_level lvl, const char *msg, void *c)
{ int e[] = { DEBUG1, LOG, WARNING, LOG }; elog(e[lvl], "%s", msg); }

dtfl_cxt = AllocSetContextCreate(TopMemoryContext, "dtfl",
    ALLOCSET_DEFAULT_SIZES);
dtfl_alloc_hooks ah = { pg_malloc, pg_realloc, pg_free, &dtfl_cxt };
dtfl_set_alloc_hooks(&ah);
dtfl_set_panic_hook(pg_panic, NULL);
dtfl_set_log_hook(pg_log, NULL);
/* no thread hooks: drive a standalone worker, as above */
.Ed
.Pp
The huge allocation functions matter:
.Fn palloc
and
.Fn repalloc
refuse more than
.Dv MaxAllocSize
(1 GB less one byte), which a large arrangement's columns exceed.
libdtfl never passes
.Dv NULL
to the realloc or free hook, which
.Fn repalloc
and
.Fn pfree
require; the
.Li p ?
test keeps the hook safe with older releases.
On an error, PG_CATCH calls
.Fn MemoryContextReset dtfl_cxt
instead of
.Fn dtfl_worker_free ,
then rebuilds.
Each
.Fn palloc
chunk is aligned to
.Dv MAXALIGN ,
which is all libdtfl's own objects need; record and difference values
are stored unaligned in any case and read with
.Xr memcpy 3 .
The context belongs to libdtfl: allocate nothing else in it and make no
child contexts under it, since the reset frees them too.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_set_alloc_hooks 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_set_log_hook 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_set_panic_hook 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_set_thread_hooks 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7
.Sh HISTORY
Rust programs choose a global allocator, panic strategy, logging facade
and thread library at link time.
These hooks give a C host the same control at run time, so libdtfl can
live inside a program that owns those facilities.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
Install all four hooks before the first
.Fn dtfl_execute .
Once installed, the panic, log and thread callbacks may run on any
worker thread and must be thread-safe.
Everything else libdtfl exposes follows the per-thread ownership
contract in
.Xr dtfl 7 .
